{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 41, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli at the age of 12. \n\n*Early Life and Reign*\nRaymond III was born around 1140. His early life is not well documented but it is known that he was educated in the court of his uncle King Baldwin III of Jerusalem. Raymond III became Count of Tripoli after his father's assassination in 1152. He was only 12 years old at the time and a regency was established to rule the county until he came of age.\n\nRaymond III was known for his military prowess and his ability to navigate the complex web of alliances and rivalries between the Crusader states. In 1153 he participated in the Siege of Ascalon alongside King Baldwin III of Jerusalem. The siege was successful and the city was captured from the Fatimids.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin*\nIn 1187 Raymond III played a key role in the Battle of Hattin. He was one of the leaders of the Crusader army that was defeated by the forces of Saladin. Raymond III fled the battlefield and escaped to Tyre. The loss at Hattin was a significant blow to the Crusader states and it led to the capture of Jerusalem by Saladin later that year.\n\n*Later Life and Legacy*\nRaymond III died in 1187 shortly after the Battle of Hattin. He was succeeded as Count of Tripoli by his godson Raymond IV of Antioch. Raymond III's legacy is complex and has been the subject of much debate among historians. Some have portrayed him as a coward for fleeing the battlefield at Hattin while others have seen him as a skilled military leader who was caught off guard by the superior forces of Saladin.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.005094549153000116}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -6.9141628955549095e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -